Commentary of al-Shinqiti (Adwa' al-Bayan) on surah 19, verse 63:

His saying, exalted is He: ﴿That is the Garden which We will cause to inherit from Our servants whoever was mindful of Allah.﴾ The demonstrative in His saying "That" [Maryam: 63] refers to what preceded it in His saying: ﴿And they will enter the Garden, and they will not be wronged at all. Gardens of perpetual residence, which the Most Merciful promised His servants in the unseen﴾, and so on to the end of the verse. In this noble verse, exalted and majestic is He, He has made clear that He causes those among His servants who are mindful of Him to inherit His Garden. He has also made this meaning clear in other places, such as:

• His saying, exalted is He: ﴿Certainly the believers have succeeded, those who are humbly submissive in their prayer… up to His saying: …Those are the inheritors, who will inherit al-Firdaws; they will abide therein eternally﴾ [al-Mu'minun: 1-11];

• and His saying: ﴿And hasten to forgiveness from your Lord and a Garden whose width is the heavens and the earth, prepared for the mindful﴾ and the verses that follow [Ali 'Imran: 133];

• and His saying, exalted is He: ﴿And those who feared their Lord will be driven to the Garden in groups﴾ and the verse that follows [al-Zumar: 71];

• and His saying: ﴿And they will be called: "This is the Garden which you have been made to inherit for what you used to do."﴾ [al-A'raf: 43];

and other verses besides these.

The meaning of their inheriting the Garden is that they are blessed with abiding in it in the most perfect bliss and joy. Al-Zamakhshari said in al-Kashshaf: "Nurith" means: We preserve the Garden for him just as we preserve for the heir the property of the deceased. Also, because the mindful will meet their Lord on the Day of Resurrection, their deeds will have ended, while their fruit remains, and that is the Garden. So when He enters them into the Garden, He has given them an inheritance from their piety, just as an heir inherits property from the deceased.

Some scholars said: The meaning of their inheriting the Garden is that Allah, exalted is He, created for every soul a dwelling place in the Garden and a dwelling place in the Fire. When the people of the Garden enter the Garden, He shows them their dwellings in the Fire, had they disbelieved and disobeyed Allah, so that their joy and delight increase. At that point they say: ﴿All praise is due to Allah, who guided us to this; and we would not have been [able] to be guided had Allah not guided us﴾ [al-A'raf: 43]. Likewise, the people of the Fire see their dwellings in the Garden, had they believed and been mindful of Allah, so that their regret and anguish increase. At that point one of them says: (p-472) ﴿If only Allah had guided me, I would have been among the mindful﴾. Then He, exalted is He, makes the dwellings of the people of the Garden available to the people of the Fire, and the dwellings of the people of the Fire available to the people of the Garden, so they inherit the dwellings of the people of the Fire in the Garden. This is the meaning of the inheritance mentioned according to this view.

His student, may Allah pardon him and forgive him, said: There has come a hadith which indicates what has been mentioned, that every person has a dwelling in the Garden and a dwelling in the Fire. However, interpreting the verse in this way is not correct, because the people of the Garden inherit from the Garden their dwellings prepared for them by their deeds and piety, as Allah, exalted is He, has said: ﴿And they will be called: "This is the Garden which you have been made to inherit for what you used to do."﴾ [al-A'raf: 43], and similar verses. And if we supposed that they inherit the dwellings of the people of the Fire, then interpreting the verse in that way would give the false impression that they have in the Garden nothing except what they inherit from the dwellings of the people of the Fire, whereas the reality is contrary to that, as you see. The hadith mentioned is what Imam Ahmad related in the Musnad, and al-Hakim in the Mustadrak, from the hadith of Abu Hurayrah: "Every person of the Garden sees his seat in the Fire and says: 'If not for Allah having guided me,' so it becomes for him a cause of gratitude; and every person of the Fire sees his seat in the Garden and says: 'If only Allah had guided me,' so it becomes for him a cause of anguish." End quote. In al-Jami' al-Saghir, al-Suyuti marked this hadith with the sign of soundness, and its commentator al-Munawi said: al-Hakim said: "It is sound according to the conditions of both (al-Bukhari and Muslim)," and al-Dhahabi affirmed this; and al-Haythami said: "The narrators of Ahmad are the narrators of the Sahih." End quote.

Commentary of al-Shinqiti (Adwa' al-Bayan) on surah 19, verse 83:

His saying, the Exalted: ﴿Have you not seen that We sent the devils upon the disbelievers, inciting them with an incitement?﴾

His saying: ﴿We sent the devils﴾ [Maryam: 83], meaning: We gave them power over them and assigned them to them as companions. This is the correct view, contrary to those who claimed that the meaning of ﴿We sent the devils﴾ is: We left them to them and did not protect them from their evil. It is said: "I sent the camel," meaning: I let it loose.

As for his saying: ﴿inciting them with an incitement﴾: al-azz (incitement), al-hazz (urging) and al-istifzaz (provoking) have the same meaning, and their meaning is agitation and intense stirring up. So his saying ﴿inciting them with an incitement﴾ means: they stir them up and drive them to disbelief and sins.

The statements of the scholars on the verse all go back to what we have mentioned. Such as the saying of Ibn 'Abbas, ﴿inciting them with an incitement﴾, meaning: they urge them on with urging; and the saying of Mujahid, ﴿inciting them with an incitement﴾, meaning: they set them on with setting on; and the saying of Qatadah, ﴿inciting them with an incitement﴾, meaning: they agitate them with agitation.

What He, Majestic and Exalted, mentioned in this noble verse, that He gave the devils power over the disbelievers and assigned them to them to lead them astray from the truth, He has explained in other places of His Book, such as:

- His saying, the Exalted: ﴿And We assigned to them companions who made attractive to them what was before them and what was behind them﴾ [Fussilat: 25];

- His saying, the Exalted: ﴿And whoever turns away from the remembrance of the Most Merciful, We appoint for him a devil, and he is to him a companion. And indeed, they avert them from the way﴾ [az-Zukhruf: 36];

- His saying, the Exalted: ﴿And on the Day He will gather them all, [and say], "O company of jinn, you have taken much from mankind"﴾ [al-An'am: 128];

- And His saying: ﴿And their brothers extend them in error; then they do not cease﴾ [al-A'raf: 202];

along with other verses.

His saying, may He be exalted: ﴿They will have no power of intercession, except one who has taken a covenant with the Most Merciful﴾ [Maryam: 87].

We have already mentioned, in the opening of this blessed book, that among the kinds of clarification (bayan) it contains is that a verse may have two or more interpretations (tafsir), all of which are true, and each of which is supported by the Qur'an. We therefore mention all of them and their evidence from the Book of Allah, the Exalted, because all of it is true. Knowing this, be aware that this noble verse belongs to that category. Some scholars said: the waw in His saying ﴿They will have no power﴾ refers to the ﴿criminals﴾ mentioned in His saying ﴿And We will drive the criminals to Hell﴾. That is: the criminals do not possess intercession, meaning they do not deserve that any intercessor should intercede for them to deliver them from the terror and punishment they are in.

This interpretation is supported by verses from the Book of Allah, such as:

- His saying, may He be exalted: ﴿So the intercession of intercessors will not benefit them﴾ [al-Muddaththir: 48];
- His saying, may He be exalted: ﴿So we have no intercessors, nor a close friend﴾ [al-Shu'ara': 100-101];
- His saying, may He be exalted: ﴿And warn them of the Approaching Day, when hearts will be at the throats, choking. The wrongdoers will have no intimate friend and no intercessor who will be obeyed﴾ — the verse [Ghafir: 18];
- His saying: ﴿And they do not intercede except for one whom He approves﴾ [al-Anbiya': 28], along with His saying: ﴿And He does not approve for His servants disbelief﴾ [al-Zumar: 7];

and other verses besides these.

From this interpretation it is understood, a fortiori, that the criminals do not intercede for others. For if they do not deserve to have others intercede for them because of their disbelief, then their intercession for others is prohibited all the more.

According to the view that the waw in ﴿They will have no power﴾ refers to the ﴿criminals﴾, the exception is disjunctive (munqati'), and ﴿man﴾ is in the accusative position. The meaning is: "But whoever has taken a covenant with the Most Merciful will have power of intercession." That is, by Allah's granting them possession of it and His permission for them, they possess intercession by what we have mentioned, and the ones for whom intercession is made deserve it by it. Allah, the Exalted, said: ﴿Who is it that can intercede with Him except by His permission?﴾ [al-Baqarah: 255]; and He said: ﴿And how many an angel in the heavens whose intercession will not avail at all except after Allah gives permission to whom He wills and approves﴾ [al-Najm: 26].

Others among the scholars said: the waw in His saying ﴿They will have no power of intercession﴾ refers to both the "righteous" (al-muttaqun) and the "criminals" mentioned in His saying: ﴿The Day We will gather the righteous to the Most Merciful as a delegation, and drive the criminals to Hell as a herd﴾. In that case, the exception in His saying ﴿except one who has taken a covenant with the Most Merciful﴾ [Maryam: 87] is connected (muttasil), and ﴿man﴾ is a substitute (badal) for the waw in ﴿they will have no power﴾. That is: none of them possesses intercession except one who has taken a covenant with the Most Merciful, and those are the believers. The covenant ('ahd) is righteous deed and the declaration that there is no god but Allah; other statements fall under this as well. That is: except the believers, for some of them will intercede for others, as Allah, the Exalted, said: ﴿On that Day, intercession will not benefit except one whom the Most Merciful has permitted and whose word He approves﴾ [Ta-Ha: 109]. Allah, the Exalted, has also made clear in other places that the objects of worship that they worship besides Allah do not possess intercession, and that whoever bears witness to the truth possesses it by Allah's permission for him in that. This is His saying, may He be exalted: ﴿And those they invoke besides Him do not possess intercession, except one who bears witness to the truth﴾ — the verse [al-Zukhruf: 86]. That is: but whoever bears witness to the truth will intercede by Allah's permission for him in that. Allah, the Exalted, also said: ﴿And on the Day the Hour is established, the criminals will despair, and they will have no intercessors among their partners﴾ — the verse [al-Rum: 12-13]; and He said: ﴿And they say, "These are our intercessors with Allah." Say, "Do you inform Allah of what He does not know?"﴾ — the verse [Yunus: 18]. The reports about intercession and its kinds are numerous and well known. And knowledge is with Allah, the Exalted.

Regarding the grammatical analysis (i'rab) of the clause ﴿They will have no power﴾, there are two views. The first: it is a circumstantial clause (hal), meaning: "We drive the criminals to Hell while they have no power of intercession," or "We gather the righteous and drive the criminals while they have no power of intercession, except one among them who has taken a covenant with the Most Merciful." The second: it is a resumptive, informative clause (mustanafah li-l-ikhbar), related by Abu Hayyan in al-Bahr [al-Muhit].

Among the scholars' views on the covenant mentioned in the verse: that it is safeguarding the five daily prayers. Whoever said this relied on the hadith of 'Ubadah ibn al-Samit, which we cited when discussing His saying, may He be exalted: ﴿Then there succeeded them successors﴾ — the verse [Maryam: 59].

Some said: the covenant mentioned is that the servant says every morning and evening: "O Allah, Originator of the heavens and the earth, Knower of the unseen and the witnessed, I make a covenant with You in this life that I bear witness that there is no god but You alone, with no partner, and that Muhammad is Your servant and Your messenger. So do not leave me to myself, for if You leave me to myself, You distance me from good and bring me near to evil. I trust only in Your mercy, so make for me with You a covenant that You will fulfill for me on the Day of Resurrection, for You do not break Your promise." When he says that, Allah sets a seal upon it and places it beneath the Throne. When the Day of Resurrection comes, a caller will call out: "Where are those who have a covenant with Allah?" So he will stand and enter Paradise. End of quotation. al-Qurtubi mentioned it with this wording as a marfu' report from Ibn Mas'ud. Sahib al-Durr al-Manthur mentioned that Ibn Abi Shaybah, Ibn Abi Hatim, al-Tabarani, and al-Hakim (who authenticated it) and Ibn Mardawayh narrated it from Ibn Mas'ud as a mawquf report (ending with him), and it does not contain the phrase "When he says that..." etc. Sahib al-Durr al-Manthur also mentioned that al-Hakim al-Tirmidhi narrated something similar as marfu' from Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him. The apparent position is that the marfu' report is not authentic. What appears to me is that the covenant in the verse includes faith in Allah, carrying out His command, and avoiding His prohibitions. This differs from the one who claimed that the covenant in the verse is like the Arab saying: "The commander ('ahida) entrusted (ila) so-and-so with such-and-such," meaning he ordered him to do it, so that the meaning would be: none intercedes except one whom Allah has commanded to intercede. This view, though correct in itself, is not correct as the intended meaning of the verse. Verses from the Book of Allah have established its correctness in general, such as:

- His saying, may He be exalted: ﴿Who is it that can intercede with Him except by His permission?﴾ [al-Baqarah: 255];
- His saying: ﴿And how many an angel in the heavens whose intercession will not avail at all except after Allah gives permission to whom He wills and approves﴾ [al-Najm: 26];
- His saying: ﴿And intercession with Him does not benefit except for one whom He has permitted﴾ [Saba': 23];
- His saying: ﴿On that Day, intercession will not benefit except one whom the Most Merciful has permitted﴾ — the verse [Ta-Ha: 109];
- His saying, may He be exalted: ﴿And they said, "Allah has taken a son"﴾ — the verses [al-Baqarah: 116];

We have discussed these and the verses with similar meaning in the Qur'an in several places, so repeating it here is unnecessary.
